  "rationale": "Shortest plan (8 techniques): 1) phish_workstation gives foothold_workstation and stolen_user. 2) pivot_internal gives reach_internal (no segmentation is deployed). 3) auth_ciserver gives foothold_ciserver. 4) escalate_ciserver gives admin_ciserver (not patched). 5) leak_secrets_ciserver gives stolen_dbadmin. 6) auth_db gives foothold_db (no MFA). 7) escalate_db gives admin_db. 8) exfiltrate_db gives exfiltrated. Why fewer is impossible: exfiltration needs an exfiltrate step, an escalate step and an auth step on db, fileserver or backup. That auth needs both stolen_dbadmin and reach_internal. stolen_dbadmin needs a leak or dump step, which needs admin on ciserver, dc or adfs, which needs an auth step plus an escalate step. reach_internal needs pivot_internal, which needs an initial foothold. The internal auth also needs stolen_user, which phishing supplies in the same step as the foothold. That is at least 8 steps. The DC and ADFS routes are longer because they also need kerberoasting through an app, API or CI server foothold."
